1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Frederik Christian Prætorius was born in about 1752.  	In December 1784, he was living at 7 King’s Street, Christiansted in the house of the Customs Director, Prætorius.  	He was appointed by a Royal Resolution of 
2 CONC 18 October 1786, as a Royal Clerk on St. Croix. 	In 1789, he was appointed as the Royal Clerk at the Weighing House on St. Croix.    In the same year, he was also a Royal Clerk in the Cashier’s Office.   	Between 1789 and 1799, he
2 CONC  held positions in the Bookkeeper’s office, the Stamped Paper office and as the Weigh Master, all in Christiansted.   	He died on 27 November 1801 on St. Thomas.   (RA, Dressel, “Personalhistoriske Samling om Dansk Vestindien,”  (
2 CONC custodian R. H. Rasmussen), Frid. Christ. Prætorius.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hypothesis, most likelys this person: Unknown Prætorius had a relationship with Ann Carroll, who was born on St. Croix in about 1761, was baptized in the Anglican Church, and was classified as a mulatto.   	 	They had a relationsh
2 CONC ip between about 1792 and 1793.   	In 1815, she was living at estate Salt River, Queen’s Quarter, and supported himself by raising eggs.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Child of unknown Prætorius and Ann Carroll:
2 CONT
